{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime-change bias: when the last 5–10 days show a clear downside break (lower lows / accelerating drop), the model stays anchored to ~70–73 and overpredicts. Apply a bearish level-shift: subtract ~2–4 EUR for days 1–10 and ~5–9 EUR for days 11–30 in such setups.",
    "Horizon drift: forecast error grows with horizon (small near-term, large long-term). Use a horizon-dependent dampener that pulls the path toward the recent trend/spot: increase mean-reversion strength with lead time (e.g., 1x for days 1–5, 1.5x for days 6–15, 2x for days 16–30).",
    "Failure to capture post-peak selloffs: after a local peak/spike in history (e.g., ~71) the true series often mean-reverts sharply lower within 2–3 weeks, but the model projects persistence/uptrend. Add an “overshoot correction”: if recent max is within last 7 days and momentum turns negative, cap any forecast increases and impose a declining slope over days 10–30.",
    "Lag in trend reversal: the model reacts too slowly to turning points (keeps rising/flat while true turns down). Add a reversal trigger: if 3-day return < 0 and 10-day return < 0, force the next 10–20 forecast points to follow the recent negative slope (not flat).",
    "Over-smoothness / under-volatility: true outcomes show larger drawdowns than forecast. Inflate downside volatility in bearish regimes (widen lower tail): allow larger negative daily steps and avoid tight clustering around the mean."
  ]
}
